    "id": "news-1790673506384-9",
    "title": "MSF: Bay Regional Hospital Receives 69 Casualties from Baidoa Fighting",
    "original_title": "Isbitaalka Gobolka Bay Oo Qaabilay 69 Qof Oo Dagaalkii Baydhabo Waxyeello Ka Soo Gaartay",
    "slug": "msf-bay-regional-hospital-receives-69-casualties-from-baidoa-fighting",
    "summary": "Médecins Sans Frontières reports that Bay Regional Hospital in Baidoa treated 69 people injured during clashes in the city on 28 September, including five children, with three deaths confirmed.",
    "content": "Médecins Sans Frontières (MSF) has reported that Bay Regional Hospital in Baidoa received 69 individuals wounded in fighting that erupted in the city on 28 September. Among the injured were five children, and three people were confirmed dead. MSF noted that these figures reflect only those who reached the hospital and do not represent the full toll of the clashes.\n\nThe violence in Baidoa, the capital of Bay Region and a key hub in southwestern Somalia, marks a significant disruption in an area that has experienced relative stability in recent months. While the exact cause of the fighting has not been detailed, Baidoa has previously been the site of tensions involving regional forces and armed groups. The city hosts a large displaced population and serves as a logistical center for humanitarian operations.\n\nDuring the fighting, some roads leading to medical facilities were blocked, according to MSF, complicating access to care for the wounded. This obstruction likely delayed treatment and may have prevented some casualties from reaching hospitals. The full extent of the casualties remains unclear, as many affected individuals may have sought help elsewhere or been unable to travel.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the incident underscores the continued volatility in parts of the country and the importance of supporting local health infrastructure. Many diaspora members have family in Baidoa and the surrounding region, and remittances often help cover medical expenses. The targeting or obstruction of medical facilities is a particular concern, as it directly impacts civilian safety and the ability of aid groups to operate.\n\nMSF’s statement highlights the critical role of Bay Regional Hospital as a primary trauma center in the region. The organization has long provided medical care in Baidoa and other parts of Somalia, often filling gaps in a fragile health system. The recent influx of wounded patients places additional strain on limited resources, including beds, supplies, and staff.\n\nAs the situation remains fluid, the diaspora is urged to verify information through credible sources and to support humanitarian efforts where possible. The full impact of the clashes may become clearer in the coming days as more details emerge.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/29/isbitaalka-gobolka-bay-oo-qaabilay-69-qof-oo-dagaalkii-baydhabo-waxyeello-ka-soo-gaartay/",
